---
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';
---
<Page
  title="An AI with governed access to Power BI · Wilson Mota"
  description="Claude reads a Power BI semantic model through Microsoft's MCP endpoint, read-only, on a tenant built from scratch. What it took, what broke, and what the first queries found."
  back={{ href: '/#bi', label: 'Business Intelligence' }}
>
  <section class="sec hd" aria-labelledby="t">
    <div class="lab"><p class="meta kick">Case study · Business Intelligence</p></div>
    <div>
      <h1 id="t">An AI with governed access to Power BI. <em>Read-only, by design.</em></h1>
      <p class="facts"><span class="live">Live</span><span>Read-only</span><span>A masked CRM dataset</span><span>Set up on 17 October 2026</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="proof" role="list">
      <div role="listitem"><p class="v">1 day</p><p class="l">From a new Microsoft tenant to the first query answered.</p></div>
      <div role="listitem"><p class="v">0 secrets</p><p class="l">The app has no client secret. Claude signs in as me, with read scopes only.</p></div>
      <div role="listitem"><p class="v">12 tables</p><p class="l">And 100 measures, read from the model's schema on the first call.</p></div>
      <div role="listitem"><p class="v">1 broken measure</p><p class="l">Found by Claude's first queries, before any report was opened.</p></div>
      <div role="listitem"><p class="v">3 tools</p><p class="l">Execute a query, read the schema, read a report's metadata. A fourth stays off.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="proof-sum">The AI never touches the data. <span>It asks the model, which answers under my permissions.</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-q">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-q">The question</h2></div>
    <div class="read">
      <p>Everyone wants an AI "on top of" their reports. The hard part is not the AI; it is giving it access that a regulated company could sign off: read-only, scoped, under a named user, with nothing stored in between.</p>
      <p><strong>So I built that access from scratch, on this domain, and let Claude use it.</strong> The model is a CRM report of mine, with the numbers masked.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-h">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-h">How it works</h2></div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="h">Four pieces, and none of them is custom code.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><b>A tenant of my own.</b><span>A Power BI account on this domain, with the tenant setting "Power BI Model Context Protocol server endpoints" switched on.</span></li>
        <li><b>An app registered in Entra.</b><span>Single tenant, a public client with no secret, delegated read permissions on datasets and workspaces, and admin consent.</span></li>
        <li><b>A connector in Claude.</b><span>Pointed at Microsoft's endpoint, <code>api.fabric.microsoft.com/v1/mcp/powerbi</code>, with that app's client ID. Sign-in is single sign-on; no password is typed anywhere.</span></li>
        <li><b>A model in a shared workspace.</b><span>The report, published to a workspace of the Pro type. Claude writes the DAX and runs it; the model answers with my permissions, so row-level security would apply.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-w" id="who">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-w">Who did what</h2></div>
    <div class="voices">
      <div class="me">
        <h3>What I decided and did</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Chose to go straight to a live connection, not screenshots: it is the version an interviewer can watch.</li>
          <li>Created the Power BI account, started the trial, created the workspace and published the model.</li>
          <li>Added the connector in Claude and authorised every tenant change before it was made.</li>
          <li>Decided what the data would be: my own CRM report, masked.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="ai">
        <h3>What the AI did <i>Claude</i></h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Read Microsoft's documentation and planned the four pieces.</li>
          <li>Registered the Entra app and set the tenant setting, each step with my permission.</li>
          <li>Diagnosed the failure below and proposed the fix that worked.</li>
          <li>Ran the first queries and checked them against the report.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-b">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-b">What broke</h2></div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="h">The schema came through. Every query failed.</h2>
      <div class="read">
        <p>From my personal workspace, reading the model's structure worked at once. But every query, even <code>EVALUATE ROW("Test", 1)</code>, came back with "Couldn't reach Power BI". Starting the Premium per-user trial changed nothing.</p>
        <p><strong>What fixed it:</strong> publishing the same report to a shared workspace of the Pro type. There, the first query answered immediately, and the old model kept failing. Microsoft's documentation does not explain why. The observation is recorded as it is.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-f">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-f">First results</h2></div>
    <ul class="lim">
      <li><b>A consistency check</b><span>Revenue by product, summed, equals the model's own [Overall Revenue] measure to the cent.</span></li>
      <li><b>A broken measure</b><span>[Num FTDs] fails: it compares a text column with numbers. It is one of the report's known faults, found by a query rather than by opening a page.</span></li>
      <li><b>A number still to reconcile</b><span>[Active Players] returns 597 with no filters, while the report page shows 1,645. The page's filters are the likely cause. Not settled yet.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-l" id="limits">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-l">Limitations</h2></div>
    <ul class="lim">
      <li><b>Not public</b><span>A visitor cannot open it: the connection needs their own Claude and their own Microsoft access. It is shown live in conversations, and as a recording here, later.</span></li>
      <li><b>A trial</b><span>The licence that makes it work ends on 16 December 2026. After that it needs a paid licence, or the live demo stops.</span></li>
      <li><b>One tool off</b><span>Microsoft's "generate query" tool needs Copilot, so it stays disabled. Claude writes the DAX itself.</span></li>
      <li><b>Masked data</b><span>The numbers are not business figures. The structure, the measures and the faults are real.</span></li>
      <li><b>Preview</b><span>The endpoint is in preview and may change.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="s-n">
    <div class="lab"><h2 class="meta" id="s-n">Next</h2></div>
    <div class="read">
      <p>The same model, three ways: the original report, my repaired version, and a dashboard Claude builds through this connection. Recorded, so the comparison can be watched.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="foot">
    <p>Sources: the decision log of this site (17 October 2026), the tenant and app settings, and the query transcripts.</p>
  </footer>
</Page>

<style>
  .steps{margin-top:20px;counter-reset:s}
  .steps li{display:grid;grid-template-columns:36px minmax(0,1fr);gap:4px 16px;padding:16px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line2);counter-increment:s}
